<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  rev_bankaccount - every bank account the charity pays into, held once. WBS 0.4 remainder.
  TAD: docs/architecture/revitalise-grant-automation-architecture.md section 3
  ("rev_bankaccount - Tier 4" paragraph), section 3.3 (relationships and cascade), section
  3.4 Gap 2 (the retention gap this table's Applicant-parented cascade remediates), section
  6.1/6 (REV_FinanceOnly). Also docs/Import/grant-application-data-model-v0.2.md's "Bank
  Account" build-specification section (the source for rev_payeetype and rev_providerid,
  restored here - see below).

  SHAPE COPIED FROM Entities/rev_grant/Entity.xml and Entities/rev_review/Entity.xml. NO LIVE
  IMPORT for this table - V1 (well-formed, locally asserted) only, same caveat as
  rev_provider/Entity.xml's header.

  USEROWNED, AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING, NOT A DEFAULT. The Applicant->BankAccount parental
  relationship (Other/Relationships/rev_applicant.xml) declares CascadeAssign=Cascade, and a
  parent can only cascade an assignment to a child that HAS an owner - exactly the reasoning
  rev_grant/Entity.xml's own header gives for why IT is UserOwned rather than copying
  rev_errorlog/rev_setting's OrganizationOwned. rev_provider beside this table is the
  opposite case: it needs no cascade-assign target, so it stays OrganizationOwned.

  EVERY COLUMN EXCEPT THE PRIMARY NAME IS IsSecured=1. rev_name (IsSecured=0) IS A GROUND-
  TRUTHED PLATFORM LIMIT, NOT A CHOICE - CORRECTED 2026-08-23 after a live
  `ensure-schema.ps1 -Env dev` run against DEV failed table creation outright with
  0x8004f501: "The field 'rev_name' is not securable." Dataverse does not allow a table's
  PRIMARY NAME attribute to carry field-level security, full stop; this is not configurable
  and is not specific to this table. The prior header text here framed IsSecured=1 on rev_name
  as a "deliberate deviation" from this solution's usual convention (every other table -
  rev_grant, rev_review, rev_application, rev_applicant - leaves its primary name unsecured so
  a lookup, related-records pane or audit summary can render without needing column-level read
  on every related record) - that framing was itself the defect: it treated an unverified
  reading of TAD section 6.1's literal wording ("every column in this table sits in the
  REV_FinanceOnly column security profile") as a design decision, when it was never checked
  against the platform (`logs/known-failure-modes.md`,
  `platform-contract-guessed-not-groundtruthed`, x30). Leaving rev_name unsecured here is not a
  privacy gap: the value is an account nickname or a masked last four (see its own Description
  below), never the full account number, sort code or any other sensitive value - those stay on
  separate columns below, all still IsSecured=1 and released only through REV_FinanceOnly. TAD
  section 6.1's "every column" wording is corrected by an ADR-level note at that section: the
  primary name is a documented, platform-forced exception to "all", not a contradiction
  requiring a redesign.

  rev_providerid RESTORES A COLUMN THE TAD DOES NOT LIST HERE, ON EXPLICIT INSTRUCTION -
  FLAGGED FOR REVIEWER CONFIRMATION. The TAD's own rev_bankaccount column list (architecture
  section 3) gives only rev_applicantid as a lookup; v0.2's Bank Account build spec gives
  BOTH rev_applicantid... no, gives ONLY a Provider lookup ("Provider | Lookup -> Provider |
  The owning provider; set for provider accounts, blank for applicant reimbursements") plus
  the Applicant relationship in its own relationship summary. Restored here because WBS 8.4
  ("Payment names one Payee - a Bank Account - a provider account or an applicant
  reimbursement") and this same table's own rev_payeetype column are unbuildable without it:
  a "provider account, reused across grants" cannot be modelled if nothing on this table
  points back to which provider it belongs to. The TAD's own relationship table (section 3.3)
  does not list Provider->BankAccount either - this is a genuine gap between the TAD's
  column/relationship lists and what its own dependent WBS tasks require, not a restatement
  of something the TAD already said. See Other/Relationships/rev_provider.xml for the
  relationship this column belongs to.

  rev_payeetype IS FROM v0.2, NOT THE TAD - see OptionSets/rev_payeetype.xml's own header for
  the full sourcing story.
-->
<Entity>
  <Name LocalizedName="Bank Account" OriginalName="Bank Account">rev_bankaccount</Name>
  <EntityInfo>
    <entity Name="rev_bankaccount">
      <LocalizedNames>
        <LocalizedName description="Bank Account" languagecode="1033" />
      </LocalizedNames>
      <LocalizedCollectionNames>
        <LocalizedCollectionName description="Bank Accounts" languagecode="1033" />
      </LocalizedCollectionNames>
      <Descriptions>
        <Description description="Every account the charity pays into, held once - a provider's account (reused across grants) or an applicant's reimbursement account. Finance role only: every column sits in the REV_FinanceOnly column security profile except the primary name, which Dataverse does not permit to be secured (TAD section 6.1)." languagecode="1033" />
      </Descriptions>
      <attributes>
        <attribute PhysicalName="rev_name">
          <Type>nvarchar</Type>
          <Name>rev_name</Name>
          <LogicalName>rev_name</LogicalName>
          <RequiredLevel>ApplicationRequired</RequiredLevel>
          <MaxLength>100</MaxLength>
          <Format>text</Format>
          <IsAuditEnabled>1</IsAuditEnabled>
          <IsSecured>0</IsSecured>
          <displaynames>
            <displayname description="Account Nickname" languagecode="1033" />
          </displaynames>
          <Descriptions>
            <Description description="A nickname or masked last-four identifier for this account (e.g. 'Sunrise Lodge - main' or '...4321') - NEVER the full account number, which lives in rev_accountnumber. Plain text, NOT autonumber: a finance user needs to recognise the account at a glance. NOT SECURED - a table's primary name attribute cannot carry field-level security in Dataverse (0x8004f501, ground-truthed 2026-08-23; see this file's own header). The value itself is never sensitive on its own (a nickname or masked last four, never the full account number), so this is a platform limit with no privacy consequence, not an omission." languagecode="1033" />
          </Descriptions>
        </attribute>
        <attribute PhysicalName="rev_accountholdername">
          <Type>nvarchar</Type>
          <Name>rev_accountholdername</Name>
          <LogicalName>rev_accountholdername</LogicalName>
          <RequiredLevel>ApplicationRequired</RequiredLevel>
          <MaxLength>200</MaxLength>
          <Format>text</Format>
          <IsAuditEnabled>1</IsAuditEnabled>
          <IsSecured>1</IsSecured>
          <displaynames>
            <displayname description="Account Holder Name" languagecode="1033" />
          </displaynames>
          <Descriptions>
            <Description description="The name on the account - the provider's business name or the applicant's own name, depending on rev_payeetype. SECURED: financial and, for an applicant account, personal data (TAD section 6.1)." languagecode="1033" />
          </Descriptions>
        </attribute>
        <attribute PhysicalName="rev_sortcode">
          <Type>nvarchar</Type>
          <Name>rev_sortcode</Name>
          <LogicalName>rev_sortcode</LogicalName>
          <RequiredLevel>None</RequiredLevel>
          <MaxLength>20</MaxLength>
          <Format>text</Format>
          <IsAuditEnabled>1</IsAuditEnabled>
          <IsSecured>1</IsSecured>
          <displaynames>
            <displayname description="Sort Code" languagecode="1033" />
          </displaynames>
          <Descriptions>
            <Description description="The UK bank sort code. Text, not a numeric type, so a formatted value (e.g. '12-34-56') round-trips unchanged. MaxLength 20 gives headroom beyond the plain 6-digit form without inventing a validation rule no source states. SECURED: financial (TAD section 6.1)." languagecode="1033" />
          </Descriptions>
        </attribute>
        <attribute PhysicalName="rev_accountnumber">
          <Type>nvarchar</Type>
          <Name>rev_accountnumber</Name>
          <LogicalName>rev_accountnumber</LogicalName>
          <RequiredLevel>None</RequiredLevel>
          <MaxLength>34</MaxLength>
          <Format>text</Format>
          <IsAuditEnabled>1</IsAuditEnabled>
          <IsSecured>1</IsSecured>
          <displaynames>
            <displayname description="Account Number" languagecode="1033" />
          </displaynames>
          <Descriptions>
            <Description description="The full account number. MaxLength 34 gives headroom for an IBAN-length value beyond the plain 8-digit UK form - no source restricts this to UK domestic accounts specifically. SECURED: the one column rev_name exists specifically to avoid ever restating (TAD section 6.1)." languagecode="1033" />
          </Descriptions>
        </attribute>
        <attribute PhysicalName="rev_active">
          <Type>bit</Type>
          <Name>rev_active</Name>
          <LogicalName>rev_active</LogicalName>
          <RequiredLevel>None</RequiredLevel>
          <DefaultValue>1</DefaultValue>
          <IsAuditEnabled>1</IsAuditEnabled>
          <IsSecured>1</IsSecured>
          <displaynames>
            <displayname description="Active" languagecode="1033" />
          </displaynames>
          <Descriptions>
            <Description description="Whether this account is currently in use for new payments. SECURED, matching every other column on this table (TAD section 6.1) even though the value itself carries no financial detail - the blanket policy admits no per-column exception." languagecode="1033" />
          </Descriptions>
        </attribute>
        <attribute PhysicalName="rev_applicantid">
          <Type>lookup</Type>
          <Name>rev_applicantid</Name>
          <LogicalName>rev_applicantid</LogicalName>
          <RequiredLevel>None</RequiredLevel>
          <IsAuditEnabled>1</IsAuditEnabled>
          <IsSecured>1</IsSecured>
          <displaynames>
            <displayname description="Applicant" languagecode="1033" />
          </displaynames>
          <Descriptions>
            <Description description="The applicant this reimbursement account belongs to. Blank for a provider-owned account (rev_payeetype = Provider). Parental, cascade delete: this is the TAD section 3.4 Gap 2 DERIVED remediation - no source document gave Bank Account a retention rule at all, so it is parented to Applicant and removed by both the retention cascade and an erasure request, the same reasoning already applied to rev_grant/rev_review via Application. Optional/not required, unlike rev_grant.rev_applicationid: a provider account never has an applicant. Declared relationship: Other/Relationships/rev_applicant.xml. SECURED (TAD section 6.1)." languagecode="1033" />
          </Descriptions>
        </attribute>
        <attribute PhysicalName="rev_providerid">
          <Type>lookup</Type>
          <Name>rev_providerid</Name>
          <LogicalName>rev_providerid</LogicalName>
          <RequiredLevel>None</RequiredLevel>
          <IsAuditEnabled>1</IsAuditEnabled>
          <IsSecured>1</IsSecured>
          <displaynames>
            <displayname description="Provider" languagecode="1033" />
          </displaynames>
          <Descriptions>
            <Description description="The provider that owns this account, reused across grants. Blank for an applicant reimbursement account (rev_payeetype = Applicant). Referential, Restrict Delete: Provider survives and cannot be deleted while a bank account still references it (TAD section 3.3's reasoning for Provider->Grant and Provider->Payment, extended here to Provider->BankAccount). RESTORED COLUMN, NOT IN THE TAD'S OWN LIST FOR THIS TABLE - see this file's own header; flagged for reviewer confirmation. Declared relationship: Other/Relationships/rev_provider.xml. SECURED (TAD section 6.1)." languagecode="1033" />
          </Descriptions>
        </attribute>
        <attribute PhysicalName="rev_payeetype">
          <Type>picklist</Type>
          <Name>rev_payeetype</Name>
          <LogicalName>rev_payeetype</LogicalName>
          <RequiredLevel>ApplicationRequired</RequiredLevel>
          <OptionSetName>rev_payeetype</OptionSetName>
          <IsGlobal>1</IsGlobal>
          <IsAuditEnabled>1</IsAuditEnabled>
          <IsSecured>1</IsSecured>
          <displaynames>
            <displayname description="Payee Type" languagecode="1033" />
          </displaynames>
          <Descriptions>
            <Description description="Provider or Applicant - which of rev_providerid/rev_applicantid is populated (v0.2's Bank Account build spec; see OptionSets/rev_payeetype.xml). Required: a bank account row must say which shape it is before either lookup can be interpreted - JUDGEMENT CALL, flagged for reviewer confirmation, since neither source states this column is mandatory. SECURED (TAD section 6.1)." languagecode="1033" />
          </Descriptions>
        </attribute>
      </attributes>
    </entity>
  </EntityInfo>
  <EntitySetName>rev_bankaccounts</EntitySetName>
  <IsAuditEnabled>1</IsAuditEnabled>
  <IsRetrieveAuditEnabled>1</IsRetrieveAuditEnabled>
  <OwnershipType>UserOwned</OwnershipType>
  <PrimaryNameAttribute>rev_name</PrimaryNameAttribute>
  <IsActivity>0</IsActivity>
  <HasNotes>0</HasNotes>
  <HasActivities>0</HasActivities>
  <!-- NO EntityKeys. No source document requires uniqueness on any column here. -->
  <!-- THESE TWO MARKERS ARE LOAD-BEARING (IMP-0006) - present with no matching folder, exactly
       as rev_provider/Entity.xml's own comment explains: this WBS task is schema only, and a
       custom form/view is WBS 8.1/8.3's job. -->
  <FormXml />
  <SavedQueries />
</Entity>
